title: 'Cross-Study: OpenSpace, AnyTool, ClawWork, World Monitor'
summary: 'Researched 4 external repos for patterns Paradigm can learn from. Top findings: outcome-based learning metrics (OpenSpace), token cost tracking per orchestration unit (ClawWork), semantic tool retrieval pipeline (AnyTool), intelligence gap narration (World Monitor), skill lineage DAG with FIX/DERIVE/CAPTURE operations (OpenSpace), and per-subsystem staleness thresholds (World Monitor). Three convergent patterns across repos: quality tracking with decay, JSONL append-only logs, hybrid BM25+semantic search.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#orchestration'
  - '#nomination-engine'
  - '#session-work-log'
  - '#conductor'
  - '#sentinel'
  - '#tool-registry'
  - '#university'
decisions:
  - id: cross-study-repos
    decision: Selected OpenSpace, AnyTool, ClawWork, World Monitor as cross-study targets
    rationale: 'Each covers a different dimension relevant to Paradigm: skill evolution (OpenSpace), tool management at scale (AnyTool), economic accountability (ClawWork), observability architecture (World Monitor)'
  - id: top-tier-selection
    decision: Prioritized 6 top-tier takeaways from 15 total findings
    rationale: Outcome metrics, cost tracking, semantic retrieval, gap narration, lineage DAG, and staleness thresholds address real gaps in Paradigm's current architecture. Others are strong but lower urgency.
learnings:
  - Outcome-based learning (did guidance actually help?) is stronger than observation-based learning (what patterns exist?) — OpenSpace tracks selection rate, application rate, completion rate, fallback frequency per skill
  - Token cost tracking per agent/phase/session is absent from Paradigm but standard in economic agent frameworks — ClawWork's EconomicTracker is the reference implementation
  - Progressive tool filtering (BM25 → semantic embedding → LLM classification → quality penalty) scales to 10,000+ tools — AnyTool solves context pollution Paradigm will face as tool surface grows
  - Intelligence gap narration (describing what the team CANNOT see) is more actionable than generic 'missing X' warnings — World Monitor's per-source narration pattern
  - Skill versioning needs FIX/DERIVE/CAPTURE operations, not append-only — OpenSpace's DAG lineage gives notebooks a version history
  - Per-subsystem staleness thresholds prevent both false positives and false negatives in health monitoring — World Monitor assigns each of 100+ sources its own maxStaleMin
  - Quality decay curves (multiplicative penalty 0.2–1.0 on unreliable tools) appeared independently in both AnyTool and ClawWork — emerging standard pattern
  - Convergence-over-volume scoring (3 different signal types > 50 of the same type) applies directly to ripple analysis diversity weighting
tags:
  - cross-study
